    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 291031\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n330 AM PDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable conditions continue through this afternoon.\n\n* Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures return to the region \n  late this week and continue through the weekend.\n\n* Breezy, cool, and dry conditions return early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nThe flow will become more southwest over the next couple of days as \na trough over the eastern Pacific pushes toward the west coast. This \nwill bring an increase in mid and high level moisture to the region \nthrough today yielding mostly cloudy skies for much of the area. \nAdditional moisture from TS Vance will also push northward up the \nColorado River Valley bringing increasing PWs through the day today, \nbut precipitation is likely to hold off until late tonight into \nFriday. Temperatures will remain near normal through Friday. \n\nThe aforementioned moisture associated with Vance will result in PWs \nincreasing to around 1 to 2 standard deviations above normal for \nlate October, resulting in a return of shower and thunderstorm \nchances across Mohave County and portions of Clark and Lincoln \nCounty late Thursday night and continuing into Saturday; however, \nthe greatest threat of shower and thunderstorm activity does look to \nremain over northwest Arizona. The aforementioned trough will \ncontinue deepening along the West Coast, developing into a split \nflow scenario with a secondary shortwave developing near southern \nCalifornia, while the core of the trough remains over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Additional moisture is expected to be pulled into the \nregion with PWs generally around 200-250% of normal for this time of \nyear. The one thing we will have to watch is where the secondary \nshortwave off the southern California coast ends up, which would \ndetermine what areas have the better chance of seeing widespread \nprecipitation. The EC is the most bullish model with widespread \nprecipitation developing over much of the southern and eastern areas \nof the forecast area Saturday afternoon into Sunday. The GFS is \ncurrently slower with the trough progression and really keeps much \nof the moisture into the far eastern areas of the forecast area and \nespecially Arizona. This also leads to plenty of uncertainty in just \nhow much precipitation will occur, with the current forecast ranging \nfrom around a quarter to a half inch across lower elevations, and up \nto an inch and a half in the mountains and localized spots across \nMohave and eastern Lincoln counties, where precipitation will be \nprolonged. Again, any change to the overall trajectory of this \nsystem could significantly alter precipitation amounts. \n\nMountain snow is also a possibility with this system, but with the \ninitial southwest flow, snow levels are expected to remain around \n8500 feet or higher, with the potential of seeing a few inches of \nsnow above 9000 feet. However, snow levels are expected to drop to \naround 7500 feet by Sunday evening, but at this time, most of the \nprecipitation is expected to have pushed east of the area with only \nlight accumulations expected. \n\nAs the trough departs the region early next week, a tightening  \ngradient on its western periphery is expected to result in breezy\nto gusty conditions on Monday, especially across the southern \nGreat Basin. Gusty conditions are expected to diminish through \nMonday evening for most, though terrain-enhanced gusts through the\nLower Colorado River Valley may persist into Tuesday. Cooler \ntemperatures will also stick around early next week, in spite of \nshortwave ridging progged to build over the region in the wake of \nthe departing trough. Highs will remain around 5 degrees below \nnormal for most through Tuesday with dry conditions expected.\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Overall, \nwinds will be light and follow typical diurnal variations through \nthe TAF period. Speeds for the most part should remain below 8 \nknots; however, an occasional, short-lived gust to 15 knots cannot \nbe ruled out later this afternoon. VFR conditions will prevail into \nFriday, with SCT to BKN clouds and bases remaining AOA 15kft AGL.    \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light winds will persist \nacross the region through the morning. Most areas will see a \ncontinuation of light winds this afternoon, although occasional \ngusts to 20 knots will be seen at times in the Owens Valley and \nColorado River Valley. All areas should see winds diminish after \nsunset and remain light overnight. VFR conditions will prevail into \nFriday, with SCT to BKN clouds and bases generally remaining AOA \n15kft AGL.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow/Phillipson\nAVIATION...Planz\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
